  "raw": " detect it quickly. Such clarifications may be influenced by patient history of symptoms or laboratory determinations during the course of the infection. In many cases such information however is not available or varies greatly from one individual to another. Such clarifying forms may take much effort from patients, physicians and laboratory personnel and would of course delay diagnosis at crucial moments that are life threatening, in spite of accurate results obtained. Such clarifier cannot entirely prevent erroneous diagnoses due to complications caused by identification of IgE screening antibodies during acute phase, despite very precise follow-up. Such clarifiers generally result in biases due to source specificity of data sources (for example, in blood, semen, pleural cultures etc.) which admittedly leads to disquieting unforeseen consequences resulting of data omission as follows:\n(a) Excess test orders subsidize treatment efforts;    (b) Clinicians order too early diagnostic tests that could have been ordered later; consequently x-ray films, samples and vital signs destructed by",
